.SH "ddc_server.opt FILE SYNTAX"
.IX Header "ddc_server.opt FILE SYNTAX"
This manpage describes the syntax of \fIddc_server.opt\fR
server option files (\*(L"server-opt files\*(R") used by the \s-1DDC\s0 corpus indexing system.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 2
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Option Processor Directives
\& 
\& #comment
\& //comment
\& include "OPTFILE"
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Logging Options
\& 
\& LogLevel LEVEL
\& SlowQueryTimeout SECONDS
\& #StatsWindow SECONDS
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Client\-Thread and Parallelization Options
\& 
\& NumThreads NTHREADS
\& ThreadQueueSize QSIZE
\& HitStringsParallel BOOL
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Navigation Cache Options
\& 
\& NavCacheSize SIZE
\& NavCacheStep STEP
\&
\& ##\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
\& ## Compatibility Options
\& 
\& VersionCompat VERSION
.Ve
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
This section contains descriptions of the various options and flags
which may occur in a \s-1DDC\s0 server opt-file as read by the
\&\f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR program versions v2.1.0 and later.
Except where otherwise noted, options are supported by \s-1DDC\s0 versions v2.1.0 and later.
.PP
Note that corpus option-files (\fI\s-1CORPUS\s0.opt\fR) are documented independently;
see \fIddc_opt\fR\|(5) for details.
.SS "Option Processor Directives"
.IX Subsection "Option Processor Directives"
\fI#comment\fR
.IX Subsection "#comment"
.PP
\fI//comment\fR
.IX Subsection "//comment"
.PP
Comments are lines starting with a literal hash-mark (\f(CW\*(C`#\*(C'\fR) or two adjacent slashes (\f(CW\*(C`//\*(C'\fR).
Such lines are ignored by the option processor.
.PP
\fIinclude\fR
.IX Subsection "include"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& include "OPTFILE"
.Ve
.PP
The \f(CW\*(C`include\*(C'\fR directive reads in the contents of an external server-opt file at the
current position.  If \s-1OPTFILE\s0 is a relative pathname, it is interpreted relative
to the directory containing the file currently being parsed.
.SS "Logging Options"
.IX Subsection "Logging Options"
The following options control the verbosity of server log messages.
.PP
\fILogLevel\fR
.IX Subsection "LogLevel"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& LogLevel LEVEL
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: LogLevel, Log, Verbose
.PP
Set the default maximum log-level for server messages to \fI\s-1LEVEL\s0\fR,
which should be one of the following:
.IP "silent" 4
.IX Item "silent"
Suppress (almost) all log messages.
Aliaes: silent, none, no, off.
.IP "error" 4
.IX Item "error"
Log only error messages.
Aliases: error, errors.
.IP "warning" 4
.IX Item "warning"
Log error and warning messages.
Aliases: warn, warning, warnings.
.IP "info" 4
.IX Item "info"
Log errors, warnings, and basic info messages.
Aliases: info, default, weak, light.
.IP "debug" 4
.IX Item "debug"
Log errors, warning, info, and debugging messages.
Aliases: debug, medium.
.IP "trace" 4
.IX Item "trace"
Log everything we can.
Aliases: trace, heavy.
.PP
\fISlowQueryTimeout\fR
.IX Subsection "SlowQueryTimeout"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& SlowQueryTimeout SECONDS
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: SlowQueryTime
.PP
Default: 10
.PP
Log any requests whose processing requires at least \fI\s-1SECONDS\s0\fR second(s) as \*(L"slow queries\*(R".
\&\fI\s-1SECONDS\s0\fR is parsed as a double-precision float, so non-integral numbers can be specified,
but will only be meaningful if \s-1DDC\s0 was compiled with high-resolution clock support \*(-- in
this case, your \s-1DDC\s0 logs should show nanosecond resolution.
.PP
\fIStatsWindow\fR
.IX Subsection "StatsWindow"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& StatsWindow SECONDS
.Ve
.PP
\&\fB\s-1OBSOLETE\s0\fR
.PP
Aliases: StatWindow, MovingAverageWindow
.PP
Default: 600
.PP
Size of the time window for tracking moving average statistics, in seconds.
Used by \s-1DDC\s0 <= v2.1.14 for tracking average query times (\f(CW\*(C`qtavg\*(C'\fR) using
a simple moving average.  As of \s-1DDC\s0 v2.1.15, average query times are
tracked using an efficent exponential moving average with delay intervals (\f(CW\*(C`qtavgs\*(C'\fR)
of 5, 15, and 60 minutes.
.PP
Since v2.1.1, until v2.1.14.
.SS "Client-Thread and Parallelization Options"
.IX Subsection "Client-Thread and Parallelization Options"
The following options control the behavior of per-client \*(L"worker\*(R" threads.
.PP
\fINumThreads\fR
.IX Subsection "NumThreads"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NumThreads NTHREADS
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: NumThreads, NThreads, Threads, NumWorkers, NWorkers, Workers
.PP
Default: 0
.PP
Specifies the number of client worker threads to be used.
If \fI\s-1NTHREADS\s0\fR is zero (the default), no client
worker-threads are created and \f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR runs in pure serial mode,
as for \s-1DDC\s0 < v2.1.0.
Otherwise,
\&\fI\s-1NTHREADS\s0\fR worker threads will be initialized on server startup for
each server node (branch\- or leaf-server), in addition to the
\&\*(L"main\*(R" server threads themselves (which are always allocated),
and \f(CW\*(C`ddc_daemon\*(C'\fR will be started in \*(L"client-thread\*(R" mode.
In this mode, the \*(L"main\*(R" server threads are only responsible
for accepting incoming client connections and enqueueing them
for later processing by a worker thread, so that up to
\&\fI\s-1NTHREADS\s0\fR simultaneous client connections can be handled
in parallel.
.PP
\fIThreadQueueSize\fR
.IX Subsection "ThreadQueueSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& ThreadQueueSize QSIZE
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: ThreadQueueSize, QueueSize, QSize
.PP
Default: 256
.PP
Specifies the maximum number of pending client connections in the
work queue in client-thread mode (see \*(L"NumThreads\*(R").
If all worker threads are occupied
and \fI\s-1QSIZE\s0\fR additional client connections have been accepted and
enqueued for processing, then the main accepting server thread will block
until the queue shrinks below \fI\s-1QSIZE\s0\fR before enqueueing any new client
connection \*(-- this basically just pushes the burden of client-queueing
beyond \fI\s-1QSIZE\s0\fR to the \s-1TCP\s0 stack.
.PP
\fIHitStringsParallel\fR
.IX Subsection "HitStringsParallel"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& HitStringsParallel BOOL
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: HitStringsParallel ParallelHitStrings
.PP
Default: false
.PP
If specified and true, branch servers created by this process will send
\&\f(CW\*(C`get_hit_strings\*(C'\fR requests to their immediate
daughters in parallel.  If the daughters need to fetch hit data from secondary
storage (i.e. if you don't have excessive amounts \s-1RAM\s0), enabling this option
is likely to actually slow things down unless your secondary storage device
supports ultra-low-latency random access reads, in which case it might help.
The parameter \f(CW\*(C`BOOL\*(C'\fR is any string recoginized as a Boolean value;
see \*(L"Boolean Flags and Switches\*(R" in ddc_opt for details.
.PP
Since v2.1.12.
.SS "Navigation Cache Options"
.IX Subsection "Navigation Cache Options"
The following options control the behavior of the branch server navigation hint cache.
Introduced in ddc v2.1.9, the navigation hint cache is an \s-1LRU\s0 cache used
by \f(CW\*(C`CDDCBranchServer\*(C'\fR nodes to minimize bandwidth requirements and
memory consumption for sequential requests through all hits for a single
query string, typically with monotonically increasing offsets and
result-sets of constant size.
.PP
\fINavCacheSize\fR
.IX Subsection "NavCacheSize"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NavCacheSize SIZE
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: NavCacheSize, NavCacheCapacity
.PP
Set the default branch-server navigation hint cache capacity to \f(CW\*(C`SIZE\*(C'\fR
(default=1024).
.PP
Since v2.1.9.
.PP
\fINavCacheStep\fR
.IX Subsection "NavCacheStep"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& NavCacheStep STEP
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: NavCacheStep, NavCacheStepSize
.PP
Set the minimum offset increment for branch server navigation cache updates to \f(CW\*(C`STEP\*(C'\fR
(default=1000).
If \f(CW\*(C`STEP\*(C'\fR is zero, a branch server's navigation hint cache will be updated for each page of hits retrieved.
Otherwise, the cache will only be updated with a new entry if the
difference between the candidate entry's offset and the maximum offset already
cached for the associated query is greater than or equal to \f(CW\*(C`STEP\*(C'\fR.
Setting \f(CW\*(C`STEP\*(C'\fR to a value greater than the default initial page size (usually 10)
should serve to minimize cache turnover and increase the life expectancy of \*(L"real\*(R"
hit-paging operations without the heavy performance penalties of ddc <= v2.1.8.
.PP
Since v2.1.11.
.SS "Compatibility Options"
.IX Subsection "Compatibility Options"
The following compatibility options are supported:
.PP
\fIVersionCompat\fR
.IX Subsection "VersionCompat"
.PP
.Vb 1
\& VersionCompat VERSION
.Ve
.PP
Aliases: VersionCompat, CompatVersion, Compatibility, Compat
.PP
Simulate the behavior of a server running \s-1DDC\s0 version \f(CW\*(C`VERSION\*(C'\fR.
In particular, a branch server with running in version \f(CW\*(C`VERSION\*(C'\fR
compatibility mode should \fB\s-1NOT\s0\fR send any requests to its immediate
daughter corpora which cannot be handled by \s-1DDC\s0 version \f(CW\*(C`VERSION\*(C'\fR.
By default, \f(CW\*(C`VERSION\*(C'\fR is the version of \s-1DDC\s0 used by the running
server process.
.PP
Useful values:
.ie n .IP """VERSION"" <= 2.1.8" 4
.el .IP "\f(CWVERSION\fR <= 2.1.8" 4
.IX Item "VERSION <= 2.1.8"
Prevents server from sending navigation hints
in generated \f(CW\*(C`get_first_hits\*(C'\fR requests, or returning
them in \f(CW\*(C`get_hit_strings\*(C'\fR responses.
See \fIddc_proto\fR\|(5) for details.
.PP
Since v2.1.9.
